research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:80:| **thm-qftx-yang-mills-selfdual-action-bound** | theorem / mathematics | Prove the action lower bound by the \(F^\pm\) decomposition and the stated topological-charge identity; show equality for \(F=\pm *F\), then use Bianchi to derive the source-free Euclidean Yang–Mills equation. Carry all metric, boundary, normalization, and characteristic-class hypotheses. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:114:For BV, choose a single grading/sign convention and define fields, ghosts, antifields, antibracket, classical master action, and the allowed local functionals. In the irreducible Yang–Mills case the minimal classical master action encodes the classical action, gauge variation term \(A^*D_Ac\), and ghost-antifield term quadratic in \(c\). Prove the master equation and that its Hamiltonian vector field squares to zero by the graded Jacobi identity. State how gauge fixing is represented (for example, a canonical transformation/gauge-fixing fermion or Lagrangian submanifold) and prove only the algebraic claim actually used. Do not equate the classical master equation with a quantum measure or the quantum master equation.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:130:For a declared \(SU(2)\) or \(SU(N)\) bundle use the chosen associated bundle and its \(c_2\) convention. Prove the normalized topological charge formula and quantization from the integral characteristic class/classification assumptions; do not import \(\operatorname{tr}_{\mathrm{fund}}\) normalization without deriving the coefficient. Prove the Euclidean action inequality by decomposing \(F=F^++F^-\), identify equality with \(F=\pm *F\), then derive the classical Yang–Mills equation from Bianchi. The existence and uniqueness of a charge-one BPST representative is a separate source-backed statement: a local worked example verifies one formula but does not classify all charge-one solutions.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:144:The one proposed open item is **op-qftx-clay-4d-yang-mills-existence-mass-gap**, classified as mathematics and nonlogical. The official Jaffe–Witten problem states: for every compact simple gauge group \(G\), prove existence of a nontrivial quantum Yang–Mills theory on \(\mathbb R^4\) with a positive mass gap \(\Delta>0\), with existence axioms at least as strong as Wightman or Osterwalder–Schrader. In that source, a mass gap means the Hamiltonian has no spectrum in \((0,\Delta)\) above the vacuum. The current Clay “Unsolved” archive still lists “Yang-Mills & the Mass Gap,” checked 2026-10-23. The item’s Status should preserve that date/source, cite partial finite-lattice/other-dimensional/regulator results without overgeneralizing, and state what remains required. It is not a dependency or a premise.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:146:The official Clay formulation does not ask for a proof of the Wilson-loop area law or color confinement. Jaffe–Witten discuss mass gap, confinement, and chiral symmetry breaking as separate desired quantum properties, then call confinement a natural extension of the prize problem. Keep these formulations separate. This scaffold does not create an op-item for “confinement” until the exact observable, group, matter content, limit, and current status have their own source review. A theorem awaiting local proof (classical Yang–Mills variation, BPST verification, local slice theorem, finite-lattice strong-coupling expansion) remains an authoring obligation and must not be mislabeled open.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:153:- Compact-lattice strong-coupling binding/area-law results are model/regime results. Do not use them as the continuum Yang–Mills mass-gap theorem.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:158:1. **Matthijs Vákár, “Principal Bundles and Gauge Theories,” arXiv:2110.06334 (2021).** Direct targeted reading of the HTML for §1.3.1, §2.1 and §2.6.5 (not the whole thesis). §1.3.1 defines principal connection one-forms through horizontal projection and the fundamental vertical action, gives the Cartan curvature \(d\omega+\tfrac12[\omega,\omega]\), and relates principal connection equivariance to the adjoint action. §2.1 defines a gauge transformation as a principal automorphism over the identity and notes the local \(M\to G\) description in a trivialization. §2.6.5 varies the gauge-field action with compactly supported variations on relatively compact open sets and obtains the covariant Yang–Mills equation. The thesis is a mathematical exposition and includes a classical gauge-field action; it does not construct a quantum Yang–Mills theory, prove a path-integral identity, or replace this category’s local proof obligations. The imported root principal-connection items cite the corresponding printed pages 32–36 and 53–54.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:160:2. **Arthur Jaffe and Edward Witten, “Quantum Yang–Mills Theory,” official Clay problem description (2000).** Read the Clay-hosted 14-page PDF, especially §§1–2, §4 “The Problem,” and §5–6. It gives the compact-group classical curvature/action conventions, distinguishes the three quantum properties mass gap, confinement and chiral symmetry breaking, defines the mass-gap spectral condition, and states the exact compact-simple-\(G\), \(\mathbb R^4\) existence-and-gap problem. Its problem text describes mathematical construction as incomplete; the separate current Clay “Unsolved Archives” page was checked 2026-10-23. This is the authoritative problem formulation/status source, not a proof that any physical model has the stated properties.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:162:3. **Glenn Barnich, Friedemann Brandt, Marc Henneaux, “Local BRST cohomology in gauge theories,” Physics Reports 338 (2000), arXiv:hep-th/0002245.** Targeted reading of the abstract, §2.2 (Yang–Mills-type field content and BRST transformations), §2.3 (nilpotence, \(H(s)\), \(H(s\mid d)\)), §2.7 (contractible nonminimal pairs), §11 (the stated cohomology theorem for Yang–Mills-type theories without free Abelian gauge symmetries, in \(n>2\)), and §12.3 (ghost-number-one anomaly classes). The text defines a specific class of local theories: matter transforms linearly in a completely reducible representation; the Lagrangian is local and gauge invariant up to a divergence; additional matter gauge symmetries are excluded for that analysis. Its local anomaly equation is \(sa+dm=0\), with counterterms removing trivial classes. The review’s theorems have technical hypotheses and selected cases; this targeted reading is not a proof reconstruction or a blanket anomaly-cancellation theorem for any gauge/matter model. The new items must reproduce the exact chosen subcase and proof locally.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:166:5. **A. A. Belavin, A. M. Polyakov, A. S. Schwartz, Yu. S. Tyupkin, “Pseudoparticle Solutions of the Yang–Mills Equations,” Physics Letters B 59 (1975), 85–87.** The full three-page primary paper was retrieved and read. In the authors’ Euclidean four-dimensional conventions, it constructs regular finite-action self-dual SU(2) charge-one pseudoparticles, identifies the charge from the asymptotic \(S^3\to SU(2)\) map, and shows that the self-dual solution saturates the action lower bound in that normalization. The paper does not provide a general moduli-space theorem and explicitly says its authors did not know whether charge \(q>1\) self-dual solutions exist at that time; do not recycle that historical uncertainty as a current open status. Translate signs and trace normalization into the library’s declared \(c_2\)/Chern–Weil convention.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:170:7. **David J. Gross and Frank Wilczek, “Ultraviolet Behavior of Non-Abelian Gauge Theories,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1343–1346; H. David Politzer, “Reliable Perturbative Results for Strong Interactions?,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1346–1349.** Read the APS abstracts only. Gross–Wilczek state that a wide class of non-Abelian gauge theories have free-field ultraviolet asymptotic behavior up to calculable logarithmic corrections. Politzer states a perturbative result for deep-Euclidean Green functions of Yang–Mills theories and specified fermionic extensions. The exact one-/higher-loop coefficients, scheme assumptions, and counterterms are not imported from the abstracts; derive or source them in the perturbative renormalization branch. These statements say nothing by themselves about the infrared or a convergent perturbation series.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:172:8. **G. ’t Hooft and M. Veltman, “Regularization and Renormalization of Gauge Fields,” Nuclear Physics B 44 (1972), 189–213.** Bibliographic record and abstract inspected; the full paper was not read for this scaffold. It presents dimensional regularization/renormalization methods for gauge fields and Yang–Mills-type theories. Cite it as a source to study, not as the proof of the new Slavnov–Taylor or anomaly statements.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:174:9. **V. N. Faddeev and V. N. Popov, “Feynman Diagrams for the Yang–Mills Field,” Physics Letters B 25 (1967), 29–30.** Metadata and abstract inspected; full text not read. The abstract refers to the extra loop contributions in Yang–Mills perturbation theory. The new local Faddeev–Popov identity must be derived from a stated slice/Jacobian setup; the source citation alone proves no local or global gauge-fixing theorem.
