research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:47:| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory-mathematics/qftx-chern-simons-bf-gravity.md**: **def-qftx-cs-bf-actions**, **prop-qftx-cs-bf-variation**, **prop-qftx-three-dimensional-gravity-bf** | A specified Chern–Simons/BF action and its classical variation with boundary terms, plus a three-dimensional gravity/BF interface. | Variation of a classical action does not quantize a theory, establish its quantum anomaly, define a boundary Hilbert space, or prove inflow cancellation. A physical boundary/interface theorem must add the field theory and boundary conditions. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:50:| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory-mathematics/qftx-categorified-line-operators.md** and **qftx-ribbon-braid-representations.md** | A KLR defect-action contract and a categorified braid/Euler interface, alongside a mathematical braid-line model. | These are specific algebraic actions and decategorifications. A mathematical braid representation is not itself a physical topological defect, and its relation to a selected QFT requires a model-specific dictionary and correlator/junction proofs. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:60:| **library/category-theory/tensor-and-fusion-categories.md**, **library/category-theory/braided-and-symmetric-monoidal-categories.md**: **def-monoidal-category**, **def-braided-monoidal-category**, **def-symmetric-monoidal-category**, **def-fusion-and-multifusion-category**; the full page item lists include coherence and fusion-rule results. | Tensor/fusion, duality, braiding, and finite semisimplicity foundations. | These are abstract categories, not defect categories of a specified QFT. Physical unitarity, defect actions, state spaces, background coupling, and junction interpretation remain local obligations. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:152:| Two October 2026 leads in **physics/research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json**: *Surface Defects in the \(\mathcal N=2\) Lens Space Index and Elliptic Integrability*, arXiv:2610.30849 (2026-10-22, INSPIRE 3217942); and *Supersymmetric Observables from Surface Defect Anomalies in 4d and 6d SCFTs*, arXiv:2610.18086 (2026-10-13, INSPIRE 3214070). | The root ledger and direct INSPIRE API responses provide metadata and abstracts only. Neither had a DOI/journal record on 2026-10-23. The first abstract concerns N=2 lens-index surface-defect difference operators and explicitly leaves a general mixed-operator contour prescription open; the second relates 4d/6d supersymmetric observables to defect anomaly polynomials. These are highly relevant source leads for the supersymmetry/defect bridge, not independently checked results or proof suppliers. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:162:5. **Supersymmetric defect anomalies.** For a named 4D \(\mathcal N=2\) or 6D \(\mathcal N=(1,0)\) SCFT and a selected half-BPS surface defect, derive the defect anomaly polynomial, supersymmetric background completion, and relation to a Casimir-energy or Rényi observable. The October 2026 arXiv lead’s abstract makes this a timely interface; the full paper and its status must be read before using any formula.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:6:      "query": "date >= 2025 and date <= 2026-10-23 and (title \"Yang-Mills\" or title confinement)",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:7:      "request_url": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature?q=date+%3E%3D+2025+and+date+%3C%3D+2026-10-23+and+%28title+%22Yang-Mills%22+or+title+confinement%29&sort=mostrecent&size=3",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:13:          "title": "Orientifold and Integrability: $\\mathcal{N}=4$ Super-Yang-Mills on $\\mathbb{RP}^4$",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:26:              "value": "We study planar $\\mathcal{N}=4$ super Yang-Mills theory (SYM) on the real projective space $\\mathbb{RP}^4$ using integrability, holography and supersymmetric localization, and discuss its relation to orientifolds in string theory. We show that the integrability of $\\mathcal{N}=4$ SYM is preserved when combining the $\\mathbb{Z}_2$ involution defining $\\mathbb{RP}^4$ with the action of the charge conjugation of the SU$(N)$ gauge group. We demonstrate this by calculating one-point functions of non-BPS single-trace operators in various subsectors at weak coupling, which can be mapped to overlaps between energy eigenstates and crosscap states in integrable spin chains. The results exhibit a parity selection rule, a hallmark of integrability, and are given in terms of a ratio of determinants. Building on these results, we conjecture a closed formula for general single-trace operators. We further provide evidence that this construction is holographically dual to a probe orientifold O1-plane in $\\text{AdS}_5\\times \\text{S}^5$ by constructing the corresponding worldsheet crosscap states in the pp-wave limit. Finally, we use supersymmetric localization to compute one-point functions of half-BPS operators exactly at finite $g_{\\rm YM}$ and $N$. The computation reduces to two-dimensional Yang-Mills theory on $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ twisted by charge conjugation and leads to a matrix integral of a Wishart-Laguerre type. In the planar limit, the exact result interpolates between the weak-coupling gauge-theory result, the strong-coupling supergravity prediction for the orientifold, and the worldsheet crosscap in the pp-wave limit.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:38:          "title": "One-loop unifying relations between Yang-Mills theory and the nonlinear sigma model from Berends-Giele currents",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:51:              "value": "We study unifying relations between Yang-Mills theory and the nonlinear sigma model. We extend tree-level amplitude relations expressed through differential operators to off-shell multiparticle currents and obtain one-loop integrand relations by sewing two off-shell legs. This off-shell approach may also apply to higher-loop integrands.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:76:              "value": "Plasma simulations for magnetic-confinement fusion (MCF) involve high-dimensional kinetic dynamics, strong nonlinear coupling, complex geometries, and multiscale spatiotemporal behavior, imposing severe computational bottlenecks. While quantum computing offers a potential alternative for accelerating plasma simulation problems, its practical advantage for fusion research has not yet been demonstrated. We review existing quantum computing studies across kinetic dynamics, wave interactions, equilibrium, and macroscopic stability, evaluating their hardware implementations and operational bottlenecks. Current studies remain largely restricted to linearized equations, reduced models, or isolated computational subroutines, leaving a substantial gap from realistic fusion simulations. We summarize key problem characteristics, including high dimensionality, structured dynamics, and measurement requirements, under which plasma applications are most likely to yield a quantum advantage. Guided by these considerations, we highlight potential approaches and present a roadmap toward practical quantum advantage of simulating plasma in MCF.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:160:              "value": "We show that choosing a test observer in $dS_D$, specified by the framed embedding of their horizon $S^{D-2}$, spontaneously breaks the de Sitter and internal sphere isometries to diagonal rotations. We identify gravitational edge modes, described by low diffeomorphism jet fluctuations near the horizon sphere, as the associated Goldstone modes. We construct their membrane-like effective theory, organized in a small-gradient expansion. We find a class of nonlinear actions that at the quadratic order reproduce the edge-mode spectrum previously found in the one-loop gravitational $S^D$ path integral.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:449:              "value": "We investigate texture zeros in the quark Yukawa matrices arising from the $\\mathbb{Z}_3$ and $S_3$ gauging of $\\mathbb{Z}_N\\times\\mathbb{Z}'_N$, associated with the non-invertible $\u0394(3N^2)$ and $\u0394(6N^2)$ symmetries, respectively. We construct the equivalence classes and their fusion rules and use them as selection rules for the Yukawa interactions. We systematically classify the full-rank quark Yukawa textures generated by the two gaugings for $N=2,3,4,$ and $5$. The two gaugings yield the same texture spectrum for $N=2$ and $N=4$, whereas the $S_3$ gauging produces additional textures for $N=3$ and $N=5$. We further analyze the most restrictive texture pairs compatible with quark data and find that there are at most nine zeros in these texture pairs. For up- and down-quark mass matrices with a total of nine texture zeros, one sector is allowed to contain six zeros while the other contains three, or one contains five zeros while the other contains four. The former case has been studied in previous works. We perform numerical fits for the latter and find it consistent with experimental data. Some viable textures also admit real quark-mass determinants while retaining a physical CKM phase, suggesting a possible connection to the strong CP problem.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/recent-source-leads.json:624:              "value": "Neutrino oscillations successfully describe flavor conversion across a wide range of experiments, yet the origin of several excesses and deficits remains unresolved. This review connects these observations and long-baseline parameter tensions to their proposed physical explanations, clarifying what remains open and how it can be tested. We examine reactor and gallium measurements, accelerator appearance and disappearance searches, and atmospheric constraints, focusing on sterile neutrinos, non-standard interactions, and decoherence in open-system dynamics. For each mechanism, we assess what it modifies, which observations it can address, and which theoretical assumptions and independent constraints limit its viability. Recent results have restricted several interpretations and weakened some earlier tensions; no single extension provides an uncontested account of all observations. We identify the flavor, baseline, energy, and event-topology signatures needed to distinguish competing explanations and assess whether the remaining anomalies require new physics or reflect unresolved flux, interaction, and detector uncertainties.",
