    "pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-euler-lagrange": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Each instance of the adopted classical pure Yang–Mills model has spacetime $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$,\ncoordinates $x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3$ of length, orientation\n$dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$, time orientation of increasing\n$x^0$, and Lorentz metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Use\n$c=\\hbar=1$, so $x^0=t$, the coordinate and spacetime lengths have unit\n$\\mathrm m$, and the natural action $S/\\hbar$ is dimensionless.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A3",
          "source": "post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "$$\n\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{YM}}(A_i)\\,d^4x\n=-\\frac{1}{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}\\,\n\\kappa(F_i\\wedge *_\\eta F_i),\n\\qquad [A_i]=\\mathrm m^{-1},\\quad [F_i]=\\mathrm m^{-2}.\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A4",
          "source": "post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For any relatively compact region on which the density is integrable, the\naction is its integral. On all of $M$, the action is defined when that\nintegral converges. Local stationarity is tested through relative action\ndifferences under smooth compactly supported adjoint-bundle-valued\none-form variations; these differences have compact support and are finite.\nNo boundary values are imposed at infinity. On a finite region, admissible\nvariations vanish near its boundary. This is an adopted classical model and\nnormalization, not a theorem, measured law, or claim that a quantum\ncontinuum theory exists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-invariant-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the axiom of countable choice. Let\n$G=SU(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=I_2,\\ \\det U=1\\}$ with its real Lie-group\nstructure. Define the Pauli matrices\n$\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$,\n$\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and\n$\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, and put\n$T_a=-\\frac{i}{2}\\sigma_a$ for $a\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$. Then $G$ is compact and\nnoncommutative, its real Lie algebra has basis $T_1,T_2,T_3$ with\n$[T_a,T_b]=\\sum_{c=1}^3\\epsilon_{abc}T_c$, and the real bilinear form\n$\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ on $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ is positive\ndefinite, symmetric, and invariant under\n$\\operatorname{Ad}_gX=gXg^{-1}$, with $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qftx-principal-connection-form",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, and let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle for a finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$, with $\\mathfrak g=\\operatorname{Lie}(G)=T_eG$. A supplied principal connection is a smooth, right-invariant complement $TP=VP\\oplus H$, where $VP=\\ker d\\pi$; write $v:TP\\to VP$ for the projection along $H$, and let $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$ be its vertical-valued curvature. For $Z\\in\\mathfrak g$ and $p\\in P$, define the fundamental vertical vector $Z_P(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_{t=0}p\\exp_G(tZ)$. The orbit differential $\\zeta_p: \\mathfrak g\\to V_pP$, $Z\\mapsto Z_P(p)$, is a linear isomorphism, as proved below. Define the **principal connection 1-form** by",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, and let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle with a principal connection form $\\omega$ associated to $H$, using $Z_P(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_0p\\exp_G(tZ)$ for the right action. For Lie-algebra-valued forms use the graded bracket defined by alternating shuffle and the Lie bracket; in degree one, $[A\\wedge B](X,Y)=[A(X),B(Y)]-[A(Y),B(X)]$. Define",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth manifold and $\\mathfrak g$ a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra. For homogeneous $\\alpha\\in\\Omega^p(M,\\mathfrak g)$ and $\\beta\\in\\Omega^q(M,\\mathfrak g)$, define their **graded bracket** by",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, $G$ a finite-dimensional real Lie group, $\\pi:P\\to M$ a smooth right principal $G$-bundle, and $\\omega$ the principal connection 1-form of a supplied horizontal distribution. Fix a finite-dimensional complex representation $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{GL}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$ that is smooth, and let $E=P\\times_\\rho V$ be its associated vector bundle with relation $[pg,v]=[p,\\rho(g)v]$. Define $\\rho_*:=d\\rho_e:\\mathfrak g\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$, where the differential is taken after viewing $\\rho$ as a smooth matrix-valued map. For any open cover with smooth local sections $s_i:U_i\\to P$, write $A_i=s_i^*\\omega$. A smooth section $\\sigma$ of $E$ has unique local components $\\psi_i:U_i\\to V$ given by $\\sigma(x)=[s_i(x),\\psi_i(x)]$. Define the **associated covariant derivative** $\\nabla^\\omega\\sigma$ by its local components",
          "uses": [
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Work in an inertial chart $x$ of the affine Minkowski model, identifying a nonempty open $\\Omega\\subseteq A$ with its image in $\\mathbb R^4$; all integrals are Riemann (Darboux) integrals. (a) If $g:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and $\\int_\\Omega g\\,\\psi\\,d^4x=0$ for every smooth compactly supported $\\psi:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$, then $g=0$ on $\\Omega$. (b) If $f:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ is $C^1$ and compactly supported and $g:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ is $C^1$, then for each $a$, $\\int_\\Omega(\\partial_af)g\\,d^4x=-\\int_\\Omega f(\\partial_ag)\\,d^4x$, provided $g$ is $C^1$ on $\\Omega$ and the products are extended by zero outside $\\operatorname{supp}f$. (c) Let $s_0>0$. If $F:(-s_0,s_0)\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous with continuous $\\partial_sF$ and there is a compact $K\\Subset\\Omega$ with $F(s,z)=0$ for $z\\notin K$ and all $s$, then $s\\mapsto\\int_\\Omega F(s,z)\\,d^4z$ is $C^1$ on $(-s_0,s_0)$ with derivative $\\int_\\Omega\\partial_sF(s,z)\\,d^4z$. The lemma is choice-free and is stated for the exact Riemann integration theory on bounded rectangles; no distribution theory, no $L^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ hypothesis and no choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "prop-adjoint-is-a-smooth-lie-group-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie group with Lie algebra\n$\\mathfrak g$. Its adjoint map is a group homomorphism",
          "uses": [
            "3.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-the-differential-of-adjoint-is-ad",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $G$ be a finite-dimensional real Lie group\nwith Lie algebra $\\mathfrak g$. Under the canonical open-subset identification",
          "uses": [
            "3.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
