batch-19.proof-contracts.json {
  "keys": [
    "version",
    "scope",
    "contracts"
  ],
  "ids": [
    "lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-invariant-form",
    "post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model",
    "pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-classical-gauge-invariance",
    "pthm-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-euler-lagrange"
  ],
  "sample": {
    "lem-qftx-su2-compact-nonabelian-invariant-form": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "ex-unitary-and-special-unitary-lie-groups",
          "source_section": "Example",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and let $n\\geq1$. Regarded as real Lie groups,\n\n$$U(n)=\\{A\\in\\operatorname{GL}_n(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I\\},\\qquad SU(n)=\\{A\\in U(n):\\det A=1\\}$$\n\nhave Lie algebras\n\n$$\\mathfrak u(n)=\\{X:X^*+X=0\\},\\qquad \\mathfrak{su}(n)=\\{X\\in\\mathfrak u(n):\\operatorname{tr}X=0\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n($\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ (Open cover, subcover, compact metric space, and compact subset of a metric space).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   (The metric topology: a set is open when every one of its points has a ball around it inside the set; closed means open complement) and bounded\n   (Bounded subset, diameter, distance from a point to a set, and distance between two sets in a metric space).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   (The absolute value makes $\\mathbb{R}$ a metric space: $d(x,y) = |x-y|$ is a metric, its open balls are the intervals $(x-r, x+r)$, and it is unbounded), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n(A complete, totally bounded metric space is compact, proved from countable choice used exactly once) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it, which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The entries of $U^*U-I_2$ and $\\det U-1$ are continuous real polynomial functions of the eight real coordinates of $U$. Their common zero set $SU(2)$ is therefore closed. Since $U^*U=I_2$, each column of $U$ has Euclidean norm one, so every matrix entry has modulus at most one; hence $SU(2)$ is bounded in $\\mathbb R^8$. By [F2], $SU(2)$ is compact. Its real Lie-group structure is supplied by [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The matrices $g=\\operatorname{diag}(i,-i)$ and\n$h=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ satisfy $g^*g=h^*h=I_2$ and\n$\\det g=\\det h=1$, so both lie in $SU(2)$. Direct multiplication gives\n$gh=\\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and\n$hg=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\-i&0\\end{pmatrix}$; therefore $gh\\ne hg$ and the group is noncommutative.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "By [F1], every element of $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ has the form\n$\\begin{pmatrix}i\\alpha&z\\\\-\\overline z&-i\\alpha\\end{pmatrix}$ with\n$\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$ and $z\\in\\mathbb C$. Thus $T_1,T_2,T_3$ form a real\nbasis. Multiplying the displayed matrices gives\n$[T_1,T_2]=T_3$, $[T_2,T_3]=T_1$, and $[T_3,T_1]=T_2$; antisymmetry and\nbilinearity give the stated $\\epsilon_{abc}$ formula.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The same finite matrix products give\n$-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(T_aT_b)=\\delta_{ab}$. Since each $X\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2)$\nhas real coordinates in this basis, $\\kappa(X,X)$ is the sum of their squares,\nand $\\kappa$ is positive definite; trace cyclicity gives symmetry. For every\n$g\\in SU(2)$,\n$\\kappa(\\operatorname{Ad}_gX,\\operatorname{Ad}_gY)\n=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(gXYg^{-1})\n=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)=\\kappa(X,Y)$, so the form is\n$\\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement fixes the nonempty matrix group SU(2)."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1: the sum-of-squares formula gives kappa(0,0)=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "There is no numerical parameter with a distinguished value one."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1: kappa(X,X)=0 forces all three real basis coordinates to vanish."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "There is no interval or endpoint parameter."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "steps 1.1 and 1.2: the matrix equations and noncommuting witnesses are explicit; Heine–Borel is choice-free."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement asserts group, Lie-algebra, and bilinear-form properties, not an equivalence."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement asserts group, Lie-algebra, and bilinear-form properties, not an equivalence."
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}

