## Example

For real $a,b\in\mathbb R$, take canonical adjoint scalar values $X^1_{\mathrm{can}}=aT_1$, $X^2_{\mathrm{can}}=bT_2$, and $X^I_{\mathrm{can}}=0$ for $I=3,4,5,6$, where $T_a=-i\sigma_a/2$. If $a,b\ne0$, the adopted N=4 scalar potential is $g^2a^2b^2/2>0$; if either coefficient is zero, it is zero.

## Example references

[[post-qftx-n4-super-yang-mills-model]]

[[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The N=4 canonical scalar potential, the SU(2) basis, and its normalized Lie bracket and invariant form.

[A1] [[post-qftx-n4-super-yang-mills-model]]: $V=\frac{g^2}{2}\sum_{I<J}\kappa([X^I_{\mathrm{can}},X^J_{\mathrm{can}}],[X^I_{\mathrm{can}},X^J_{\mathrm{can}}])$.

[F1] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: $[T_1,T_2]=T_3$ and $\kappa(T_3,T_3)=1$.

## Verification

**Given:** [A1], [F1], and $g>0$.

1.1 Bilinearity of the Lie bracket gives $[X^1_{\mathrm{can}},X^2_{\mathrm{can}}]=ab[T_1,T_2]=abT_3$. Every other commutator in the sum contains a zero field and is zero. [given, A1, F1, algebra]

1.2 Substitution into [A1] yields $V=\frac{g^2}{2}a^2b^2\kappa(T_3,T_3)=\frac{g^2a^2b^2}{2}$. Since $g>0$, this is positive exactly when both $a$ and $b$ are nonzero, and the formula gives zero on either zero-coefficient boundary. [given, A1, F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎


