+## Example
+
+Choose the fundamental representation $R=\mathbb C^2$ of $SU(2)$. In each superspace fiber let $Q$ be a column in $R$, let $\\widetilde Q$ be a row in $R^*$, and let $\\Phi$ act by the represented adjoint action. Under any chiral supergauge transformation $U=e^{i\\Lambda_R}$, set $Q'=UQ$, $\\widetilde Q'=\\widetilde Q U^{-1}$, and $\\Phi'=U\\Phi U^{-1}$. Then the mass bilinear $\\widetilde Q Q$ and cubic bilinear $\\widetilde Q\\Phi Q$ in the adopted N=2 superpotential are gauge invariant. This checks gauge invariance of these two terms only; it does not prove the extended supersymmetry variation.
+
+## Example references
+
+[[post-qftx-n2-vector-hypermultiplet-model]]
+
+[[def-qftx-n1-vector-superfield]]
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The fundamental representation and chiral supergauge transformations in the Example, under the adopted N=2 classical model.
+
+[A1] [[post-qftx-n2-vector-hypermultiplet-model]]: one hypermultiplet has chiral superfields $Q$ in $R$ and $\\widetilde Q$ in $R^*$, with superpotential $\\sqrt2\\,\\widetilde Q\\Phi Q+m\\widetilde Q Q$.
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-n1-vector-superfield]]: in a representation $R$, a chiral matter superfield transforms as $\\Phi_R\\mapsto e^{i\\Lambda_R}\\Phi_R$ under an admissible chiral supergauge parameter.
+
+## Verification
+
+**Given:** [A1], [F1], and the fundamental representation $U=e^{i\\Lambda_R}$ with its dual action on $R^*$.
+
+1.1 The dual action is right multiplication by $U^{-1}$, so $\\widetilde Q'Q'=\\widetilde Q U^{-1}UQ=\\widetilde Q Q$. Thus the mass bilinear is invariant. [given, A1, F1, algebra]
+
+1.2 The adjoint action gives $\\widetilde Q'\\Phi'Q'=\\widetilde Q U^{-1}(U\\Phi U^{-1})UQ=\\widetilde Q\\Phi Q$. Hence the cubic bilinear, and therefore the stated linear combination of mass and cubic terms, is invariant. [given, A1, F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
+
