+## Example
+
+Set the N=2 hypermultiplet representation to the adjoint and its mass to $m=0$. If $Q$ and $\\widetilde Q$ are its canonically normalized chiral fields and $\\Phi_1$ is the connection-normalized N=2 vector chiral field, define connection-normalized adjoint chiral fields $\\Phi_2=gQ$ and $\\Phi_3=g\\widetilde Q$. Then the N=2 hypermultiplet kinetic terms and cubic superpotential become exactly the two additional adjoint-chiral terms in the adopted N=4 N=1 action, and the on-shell component counts agree: one vector, three complex adjoint scalars, and four adjoint Weyl fermions. The calculation does not prove the N=4 supersymmetry variation.
+
+## Example references
+
+[[post-qftx-n2-vector-hypermultiplet-model]]
+
+[[post-qftx-n4-super-yang-mills-model]]
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The N=2 action with one hypermultiplet, the N=4 N=1 action, and the stated connection/canonical field rescaling.
+
+[A1] [[post-qftx-n2-vector-hypermultiplet-model]]: the vector chiral field is connection-normalized; the hyper kinetic terms are canonical; the hyper superpotential is $\\sqrt2\\,\\widetilde Q\\Phi_1Q+m\\widetilde Q Q$.
+
+[A2] [[post-qftx-n4-super-yang-mills-model]]: the N=4 model has one vector and three connection-normalized adjoint chirals, with action coefficient $1/g^2$ and cubic term $\\sqrt2\\,\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi_1,[\\Phi_2,\\Phi_3])$.
+
+## Verification
+
+**Given:** [A1], [A2], $R=\\mathrm{ad}$, $m=0$, and $g>0$.
+
+1.1 Substituting $Q=\\Phi_2/g$ and $\\widetilde Q=\\Phi_3/g$ into the two hypermultiplet D-terms gives $g^{-2}$ times the D-terms of $\\Phi_2$ and $\\Phi_3$ in the N=4 action. The N=2 vector term already equals the N=4 vector and $\\Phi_1$ terms by [A1] and [A2]. [given, A1, A2, algebra]
+
+1.2 With the adjoint representation, $\\widetilde Q\\Phi_1Q=\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi_3,[\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2])/g^2$. Invariance and symmetry of $\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}$ give $\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi_3,[\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2])=\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi_1,[\\Phi_2,\\Phi_3])$, so the cubic terms agree; $m=0$ removes the mass term. [given, A1, A2, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 The N=2 vector contributes one connection, one complex adjoint scalar, and two adjoint Weyl fermions; the adjoint hyper adds two complex adjoint scalars and two adjoint Weyl fermions. Thus there are three complex, hence six real, adjoint scalars and four adjoint Weyl fermions, exactly the N=4 content adopted in [A2]. [given, A1, A2, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
+
