+## Postulate
+
+Use the spacetime, orientation, time orientation, mostly-plus metric, global group $SU(2)$ (not $SO(3)$), selected smooth right-principal bundle sector, basis $T_a=-i\sigma_a/2$, invariant form $\kappa(X,Y)=-2\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, and positive dimensionless coupling $g=g_{\mathrm{YM}}$ specified for the N=2 vector/hypermultiplet model. In the N=1 decomposition, let $V_{\mathrm{ad}}$ be its adjoint vector superfield and let $\Phi_1,\Phi_2,\Phi_3$ be three adjoint chiral superfields in connection normalization. Each chiral multiplet has one complex adjoint scalar, one adjoint Weyl fermion, and an algebraic auxiliary field. The canonical chiral fields are $\Phi_{i,\mathrm{can}}=\Phi_i/g$.
+
+Write $\kappa_{\mathbb C}$ for the complex-bilinear extension of $\kappa$, $\kappa_H(\bar X,Y)=\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\bar X,Y)$, and $[X,Y]$ for the complexified Lie bracket. Use $D_\alpha=\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha}+i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$ and $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}=-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}-i\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}\partial_\mu$. Each $\Phi_i$ is an even chiral superfield satisfying $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}\Phi_i=0$, and the adjoint chiral field strength is $\mathcal W_{\alpha,\mathrm{ad}}=-\tfrac14\bar D^{\dot\beta}\bar D_{\dot\beta}(e^{-2gV_{\mathrm{ad}}}D_\alpha e^{2gV_{\mathrm{ad}}})$. The adopted connection-normalized N=1 action is
+
+$$\mathcal L_{N=4}=\frac{1}{g^2}\left\{\frac14\left([\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\mathcal W^\alpha,\mathcal W_\alpha)]_F+\mathrm{h.c.}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^3[\kappa_H(\bar\Phi_i,e^{2gV_{\mathrm{ad}}}\Phi_i)]_D+\left([\sqrt2\,\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\Phi_1,[\Phi_2,\Phi_3])]_F+\mathrm{h.c.}\right)\right\}.$$
+
+Here $[Y]_F=\int d^2\theta\,Y$ and $[Z]_D=\int d^4\theta\,Z$ use the coefficient-extraction measures specified in the superspace Grassmann algebra. In canonical fields, the cubic superpotential coefficient is $\sqrt2g$, so its Yukawa coupling equals the gauge coupling. The equivalent on-shell field content is one gauge connection, six real adjoint scalars $X^I$ ($I=1,\ldots,6$), and four adjoint Weyl fermions $\lambda^A$ ($A=1,\ldots,4$). The scalar potential in connection normalization is
+
+$$V_{\mathrm{scalar}}=\frac{1}{2g^2}\sum_{1\le I<J\le6}\kappa([X^I,X^J],[X^I,X^J]);$$
+
+for canonical fields $X^I_{\mathrm{can}}=X^I/g$ this is $\frac{g^2}{2}\sum_{I<J}\kappa([X^I_{\mathrm{can}},X^J_{\mathrm{can}}],[X^I_{\mathrm{can}},X^J_{\mathrm{can}}])$. With the N=2 model's N=1 decomposition, this is the $R=\mathrm{ad}$, $m=0$ hypermultiplet specialization after rescaling its two canonical hypermultiplet chiral fields by $g$ to the connection normalization of $\Phi_2$ and $\Phi_3$.
+
+Adopt rigid four-dimensional $\mathcal N=4$ supersymmetry, with four independent left-Weyl supercharges and sixteen real components, as part of this physical model postulate. The action's extended invariance and on-shell closure are not proved in this item. This is a classical action with smooth fields in local bundle trivializations and compactly supported admissible variations; no path-integral measure, regulator, quantum continuum theory, or empirical result is asserted. Use $c=\hbar=1$: $g$ is dimensionless, connection-normalized scalar fields have units $\mathrm m^{-1}$, and canonical fields have mass dimension one.
+
+## Postulate references
+
+[D'Hoker and Phong, §§2.4 and 2.9–2.10](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9912271v1)
+
+[Martin, §§3.4, 4.5 and 4.9](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7)
