+
+## Lemma
+
+Represent $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ by anti-Hermitian traceless $2\times2$ matrices, use the matrix-commutator bracket $[X,Y]=XY-YX$, and define $\kappa_{\mathbb C}(X,Y)=-2\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ on its complexification. Let $\Phi_1,\Phi_2,\Phi_3$ be even adjoint chiral superfields and let $\Lambda$ be an even chiral gauge parameter in the complexified Lie algebra. Under the local supergauge transformation $\Phi_i'=e^{i\Lambda}\Phi_i e^{-i\Lambda}$, the chiral polynomial $W_{\mathrm{ad}}=\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\Phi_1,[\Phi_2,\Phi_3])$ is invariant pointwise in superspace. Therefore any constant multiple of this cubic is an admissible gauge-invariant superpotential term.
+
+## Lemma references
+
+[[def-qftx-n1-vector-superfield]]
+
+[[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The matrix bracket, trace form, and simultaneous adjoint supergauge transformation in the Lemma.
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-n1-vector-superfield]]: a chiral matter superfield in representation $R$ transforms by $\Phi_R\mapsto e^{i\Lambda_R}\Phi_R$.
+
+[F2] [[post-qftx-nonabelian-yang-mills-model]]: $\kappa(X,Y)=-2\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ and this form is $\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 For matrices, direct expansion gives $[\Lambda,[X,Y]]=[[\Lambda,X],Y]+[X,[\Lambda,Y]]$. Also, cyclicity of trace gives $\kappa_{\mathbb C}([\Lambda,X],Y)+\kappa_{\mathbb C}(X,[\Lambda,Y])=-2\operatorname{Tr}_2(\Lambda XY-XY\Lambda)=0$. Hence the infinitesimal adjoint action preserves both the bracket and $\kappa_{\mathbb C}$. [given, F2, algebra]
+
+1.2 Applying these identities to $W_{\mathrm{ad}}$ shows its infinitesimal variation under $\delta_\Lambda\Phi_i=i[\Lambda,\Phi_i]$ vanishes: the variation of the bracket is $i[\Lambda,[\Phi_2,\Phi_3]]$, and invariance of $\kappa_{\mathbb C}$ cancels the resulting two terms. [given, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 Let $\delta_\Lambda$ be the derivation of the polynomial algebra on the three fields with $\delta_\Lambda\Phi_i=i[\Lambda,\Phi_i]$. Step 1.2 gives $\delta_\Lambda W_{\mathrm{ad}}=0$, so $\delta_\Lambda^kW_{\mathrm{ad}}=0$ for every $k\geq1$. The exponential $e^{\delta_\Lambda}$ acts by the finite substitution $\Phi_i\mapsto e^{i\Lambda}\Phi_i e^{-i\Lambda}$, since $e^{i\Lambda}Xe^{-i\Lambda}=e^{i\operatorname{ad}_\Lambda}X$ by the matrix-exponential power series. Hence the finite supergauge transformation leaves $W_{\mathrm{ad}}$ unchanged. [given, F1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
