+## Postulate
+
+Adopt a classical rigid four-dimensional model on the oriented, time-oriented Minkowski spacetime $M=\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^\mu$ of length, orientation $dx^0\wedge dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3$, time orientation of increasing $x^0$, and metric $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Use $c=\hbar=1$. Fix the global gauge group $G=SU(2)$, not its quotient $SO(3)$. The adjoint action is $\operatorname{Ad}_uX=uXu^{-1}$; choose one smooth right principal bundle $\pi:P\to M$ in the single sector $\Sigma=\{[P]\}$, where $[P]$ means smooth bundle isomorphism over $\operatorname{id}_M$. In the anti-Hermitian basis $T_a=-i\sigma_a/2$, use $\kappa(X,Y)=-2\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, so $\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\delta_{ab}$, and a fixed dimensionless coupling $g=g_{\mathrm{YM}}>0$. Use $\sigma^0=I_2$, the Pauli matrices $\sigma^j$, $\bar\sigma^0=I_2$, $\bar\sigma^j=-\sigma^j$, and $\epsilon_{12}=1$; there is no theta term in this selected model.
+
+Use the N=1 superspace algebra with even coordinates $x^\mu$ and odd coordinates $\theta^\alpha,\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}$, the left derivatives and Berezin measures specified in the superspace definitions, and $D_\alpha=\partial^L_{\theta^\alpha}+i\sigma^\mu_{\alpha\dot\beta}\bar\theta^{\dot\beta}\partial_\mu$, $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}=-\partial^L_{\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}}-i\theta^\beta\sigma^\mu_{\beta\dot\alpha}\partial_\mu$. A chiral field obeys $\bar D_{\dot\alpha}\Phi=0$. In each representation $R$, the dimensionless real vector prepotential $V_R$ transforms by $e^{2gV_R}\mapsto e^{i\Lambda_R^\dagger}e^{2gV_R}e^{-i\Lambda_R}$ and a chiral matter field transforms by $Q_R\mapsto e^{i\Lambda_R}Q_R$, where $\Lambda_R$ is a chiral gauge-parameter superfield. Its chiral field strength is $\mathcal W_{\alpha,R}=-\tfrac14\bar D^{\dot\beta}\bar D_{\dot\beta}(e^{-2gV_R}D_\alpha e^{2gV_R})$. The connection-normalized vector multiplet consists on shell of the connection $A_\mu$, one complex adjoint scalar $\phi$, and two adjoint Weyl fermions; its N=1 decomposition is one vector superfield $V_{\mathrm{ad}}$ and one adjoint chiral superfield $\Phi$. The canonical scalar is $\phi_{\mathrm{can}}=\phi/g$. Here “on shell” means after eliminating algebraic auxiliary fields and imposing the field equations; the full vector superfield also contains the real auxiliary adjoint field $D$.
+
+Choose one finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space $V_R$ and smooth unitary representation $\rho_R:SU(2)\to U(V_R)$. The hypermultiplet consists of chiral superfields $Q$ and $\widetilde Q$ whose scalar components are smooth sections of $P\times_{\rho_R}V_R$ and its dual; the dual action is $\rho_{R^*}(u)\ell=\ell\circ\rho_R(u^{-1})$. Each chiral N=1 hypermultiplet field has one complex scalar, one Weyl fermion, and one auxiliary field; hence the pair has two complex scalars and two Weyl fermions, which form one Dirac fermion on shell. Write $\mathfrak{su}(2)_{\mathbb C}=\mathfrak{su}(2)\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C$ and let $\kappa_{\mathbb C}$ be the complex-bilinear extension of $\kappa$. This form is nondegenerate and invariant under the complexified adjoint action, so for $R=\mathrm{ad}$ it identifies the dual with the adjoint. Use the Hermitian form $h_R(X,Y)=\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\overline X,Y)$ on the complexified adjoint, where conjugation is with respect to the real Lie algebra. The local invariant pairing is $\widetilde Q Q$, and $\widetilde Q\Phi Q$ means $\widetilde Q\,\rho_{R*}(\Phi)Q$. The hypermultiplet is canonically normalized, while $\Phi$ is in the connection normalization above. “Holomorphic” means complex analytic in the adjoint coordinates $\Phi^a$ on $\mathfrak{su}(2)_{\mathbb C}$. The microscopic vector prepotential is the holomorphic, gauge-invariant quadratic function
+
+$$\mathcal F_{\mathrm{UV}}(\Phi)=\frac{i}{2g^2}\,\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\Phi,\Phi),\qquad \operatorname{Im}(\mathcal F_{ab})=\frac{1}{g^2}\kappa_{ab}>0.$$
+
+Here $\mathcal F_{ab}=\partial_a\partial_b\mathcal F_{\mathrm{UV}}$ in the basis $T_a$. In the local Berezin normalization, its N=1 vector action is
+
+$$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{vec}}=\frac{1}{4g^2}\left([\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\mathcal W^\alpha,\mathcal W_\alpha)]_F+\mathrm{h.c.}\right)+\frac{1}{g^2}[\kappa_H(\bar\Phi,e^{2gV_{\mathrm{ad}}}\Phi)]_D,$$
+
+where $\mathcal W_{\alpha}$ is the chiral field strength and $\kappa_H(\bar X,Y)=\kappa_{\mathbb C}(\bar X,Y)$. For a superspace expression $X$, $[X]_F=\int d^2\theta\,X$ and $[X]_D=\int d^4\theta\,X$, using the coefficient-extraction measures and generator order in the superspace Grassmann algebra. Thus the vector kinetic term has exactly the connection-normalized Yang-Mills coefficient $-\kappa(F\wedge *_\eta F)/(2g^2)$.
+
+The adopted renormalizable one-hypermultiplet action is
+
+$$\mathcal L_{N=2}=\mathcal L_{\mathrm{vec}}+[Q^\dagger e^{2gV_R}Q+\widetilde Q^\dagger e^{2gV_{R^*}}\widetilde Q]_D+\left([\sqrt2\,\widetilde Q\Phi Q+m\widetilde Q Q]_F+\mathrm{h.c.}\right),$$
+
+with complex mass parameter $m$ of dimension $\mathrm m^{-1}$. In canonical normalization $\Phi_{\mathrm{can}}=\Phi/g$, the cubic coefficient is $\sqrt2g$, the same gauge coupling that appears in the covariant derivative. The ultraviolet prepotential above is quadratic; a low-energy effective prepotential may instead be non-polynomial and valid only on a specified electric/magnetic patch. This postulate adopts no such low-energy solution or duality claim.
+
+The model includes rigid four-dimensional $\mathcal N=2$ supersymmetry: “rigid” means its spinor parameters are constant in spacetime, and $\mathcal N=2$ means two independent left-Weyl supercharges, hence eight real components. Its invariance is an adopted model premise, not a deduction in this item. The N=1 superspace expression is a field/action packaging; the full hypermultiplet transformations close only after imposing the physical field equations, and the finite N=1 auxiliary fields are eliminated in this on-shell description. Fields are smooth in local trivializations and admissible variations are smooth and compactly supported; actions are taken over relatively compact regions or when the spacetime integral converges. No path-integral measure, regulator, quantum theory, or continuum existence claim is included. In mass-dimension notation $[x]=-1$, $[\theta]=-\frac12$, $[\Phi]=[Q]=[\widetilde Q]=1$, $[\mathcal W_\alpha]=3/2$, $[m]=1$, and $[g]=0$; equivalently scalar fields and mass parameters have units $\mathrm m^{-1}$.
+
+## Postulate references
+
+[D'Hoker and Phong, §§2.4, 2.9–2.10 and 3.5–3.7](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9912271v1)
+
+[Martin, §§3.4, 4.5 and 4.9](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-ph/9709356v7)
